{% doc %}
  One ratio keyword, resolved to a CSS `aspect-ratio` value.

  ## Why this is not seven strings in each caller

  The seven names are the theme's vocabulary — every image control in every
  block offers the same list — and until now the numbers behind them lived in
  two `case` blocks inside `snippets/image.liquid`. That was fine while an
  `<img>` was the only thing that had a shape. `blocks/image-card.liquid` gives
  the *card* the ratio, because its picture is a background rather than an
  element, and a third copy of "portrait means 3 / 4" is the copy that ends up
  disagreeing with the other two.

      square     1 / 1
      portrait   3 / 4
      landscape  4 / 3
      wide       16 / 9
      tall       2 / 3
      long       9 / 16
      adapt      the image's own ratio

  Anything else is passed through, so a caller with a genuine one-off can hand
  over `5 / 7` and it lands in the declaration untouched.

  @param {string} [ratio] - One of the names above, or a raw `aspect-ratio` value
  @param {image} [image] - Read for its own ratio when `ratio` is `adapt`
  @param {string} [fallback] - Used for `adapt` when the image has no ratio. Default: 1

  @example
    {%- liquid
      capture aspect
        render 'aspect-ratio', ratio: s.ratio, image: s.image
      endcapture
    -%}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign key = ratio | default: 'adapt'

  case key
    when 'square'
      assign value = '1 / 1'
    when 'portrait'
      assign value = '3 / 4'
    when 'landscape'
      assign value = '4 / 3'
    when 'wide'
      assign value = '16 / 9'
    when 'tall'
      assign value = '2 / 3'
    when 'long'
      assign value = '9 / 16'
    when 'adapt'
      assign value = image.aspect_ratio | default: fallback | default: 1
    else
      assign value = key
  endcase
-%}
{{- value -}}
